-- SUM-TYPE-FIELDS-SPEC §5 item 4: the OVERLOAD TRAP, pinned.
--
-- `rent` is a field of two UNRELATED types here, so the name resolves to two
-- selectors and the checker forks an overload candidate for each. S2's
-- diagnostic is recorded inside the surviving fork — but raising it THERE
-- would make that candidate non-viable, and `prune` would then report
-- "There are multiple definitions for the identifier `rent`" instead: an
-- ambiguity error about a name that is not actually ambiguous, in place of the
-- one message that says what is wrong.
--
-- So the obligation is parked on `CheckState.pendingPartialProjections` and
-- drained by `inferTopDecl`, the first point above every fork. The state that
-- continues is the winner's, so only the winner's obligations survive.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE ASSERTS is therefore the golden's CONTENT and not merely that
-- it is red: the message below must be S2's, naming `rent`, `Landlord` and
-- `Actor`. If it ever becomes `AmbiguousTermError` or `InternalAmbiguityError`,
-- the deferral has been undone.
--
-- (§5 item 4 first proposed exempting S2 in `viableCandidate` instead. That was
-- REPLACED: it contradicts `severity`'s documented claim to be the one place
-- that decides what blocks, and it leaves a candidate carrying a blocking
-- diagnostic viable — so two candidates go viable and `prune` collapses them
-- to `InternalAmbiguityError`, the same outcome moved one step later.)

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord
    Tenant HAS rent IS A NUMBER

DECLARE Vehicle IS ONE OF
    Car HAS rent IS A NUMBER

GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rent of` a MEANS a's rent
